 station on saturday and will spend the  next six months at the lab  before heading back to earth inside the  same spacecraft  that got them there time is running out  to find a missing indonesian submarine  which disappeared off the coast of bali  on wednesday  the sub now has less than a day's supply  of oxygen for the 53 people on board  and there are fears the vessel may have  sunk so deep that it won't be able to  handle the pressure  indonesia's navy believes an electrical  failure caused the sub to lose control  during a training exercise still ahead  those blue skies over alberta  are producing a green boom  alberta is set to lead the country in  the growth of wind and solar resources  according to canada's energy regulator  and as far nasa reports  displaced workers in the oil and gas  sector are counting on the green side  of the energy sector like many albertans  drew mayor's career has been shaped in  the boom and bust  of oil and gas but in the bust of 2015  he took a chance and then i decided it  was time for a change  an electrician by trade he turned his  skills to a new form of energy  solar it's just such a underutilized  resource that  you know shines especially in alberta  mayor charted a new course started his  own company it was slow at first but now  i'm swamped business is booming  his team is paneling roof after roof  with no bust in sight  across oil country a construction boom  with new projects of staggering scale  propelled by sunny market conditions  we're the sunshine state of the north  green gate power is developing this  immense project  that will power more than a hundred  thousand homes and employ  500 in its construction we have a lot of  skilled labor  here in alberta i believe a lot of those  skills are easily transferable to  renewable energy projects the boom is  due in part to the sliding dominance  of fossil fuels especially the drive to  phase out coal  canada's energy regulator forecasts the  province's wind power capacity will  double by 2023  and solar will soar many times higher  it is a really remarkable thing how  quickly costs have fallen for wind  and solar over the last number of years  about seven years ago in my previous  service here on the montana first nation  and mask watches  under the leadership of chief leonard  standing on the road  drew mayer now oversees the largest  solar farm on indigenous land in canada  we can do it because it's coming into  play and  just the right timing perfect timing the  farm just went online  last october and when that thing got  activated it cut our power in half  so we knew that we were onto something  it's a shining source of pride here  where wealth was once grounded in oil  until the wells went dry since then  montana has made the shift  one by one key buildings were arrayed  with solar of their own  and all feeding the grid through  montana's own renewable energy company  and for young people here a future in a  resource  that will never run out oh it's made a  big difference  in our attitude of our nation members  that they're really  looking at it as an opportunity for  careers  for mayor montana is an inspiration  wherever there's  wind and sun it just goes to show that  even though montana is a small nation  with the right people in place and the  right desire you can accomplish these  these types of projects fara nasser  global news toronto  with the third wave comes a career  crisis up next  why some healthcare workers are ready to  quit  for more than a year health care workers  have endured long  stressful shifts to care for patients  suffering with coven 19.  they're exhausted burnt out and in some  cases struggling with post-traumatic  stress  now this third and most threatening wave  is upon them  as christa hassi explains many are  rethinking their careers  and making a difficult choice so just  finishing up shift today on the covid  unit  we're starting to see our  hospitalizations climb steadily  dr nisha bakshi is the internal medicine  lead at the royal alexandra hospital in  edmonton  she's also in charge of the hospital's  covet response  dr bakshi says these days it's been hard  to fill shifts on the kovit ward  everybody's tired of it it's exhausting  to work on the covet unit  it's a grim reality as canada fights the  third wave of the pandemic  icu beds are full patients are younger  and sicker  and it's taking a toll on health care  workers dr bakshi actually holds the  record for the most days spent on the  coveted ward at her hospital  but she says it's come at a cost in  january she was diagnosed with  post-traumatic stress disorder  or ptsd i noticed that i was having  difficulty sleeping i was having a lot  of nightmares  i was reliving and replaying  conversations i had with families  she got help and now hopes others in the  medical field will do the same  none of what we went through was normal  we all showed up and did our jobs  but we're not okay and it's okay to say  that  close to 70 percent of all health care  workers reported worsening mental health  during the pandemic  according to statistics canada  psychologist melanie jones provides  counseling for healthcare workers  she says a lot of her clients are  exhausted and burnt out  people are looking at this and saying i  have nothing else to give  just as hospitals race to increase their  capacity some in the health care field  are walking away from the profession  they love  or are considering it you could create  the 5 000 physical beds  but if we don't have the right skilled  staff we will quickly get overwhelmed  how worried are you about hospitals  capacity to treat people during the  third wave  and into the future acutely right now  i'm very worried so i think you're we're  going to have a  a challenge with people with just enough  people to to run the show  christa hessie global news toronto  for more on the wave of covet fatigue  hitting canadian healthcare you can  watch the new reality with donna friesen  tomorrow night at 7  right here on global so many health care  workers are going above and beyond right  now including nurse amy lin  in ottawa  [Music]  she was just transferred to a struggling  icu and brought along her guitar to race  spirits  so we leave you tonight with more of her  serenade to patience  thank you for watching and i hope to see  you back here tomorrow good  not  night  you are not alone
 

